usb: typec: add a driver for the Maxim MAX77705 CCIC

The MAX77705 companion PMIC has a Type-C and USB PD controller (CCIC) at
its own I2C address, 0x25. Detection and the PD policy engine run in the
CCIC's firmware; the host reads CC and PD status registers and sends
commands through a mailbox: [opcode, data] from 0x21, a write to 0x41 to
start, the APCmdRes interrupt, then the echo and response from 0x51.
Mainline has drivers for the chip's top, charger, gauge, LEDs and haptic,
but nothing for the CCIC, so on the Galaxy Tab S8+ (SM-X800) VBUS was a
manual userspace switch, dwc3 was fixed in host mode, and a PD charger
stayed at its default 5 V contract.

This driver, modelled on rt1719.c:

- registers a DRP/DRD typec port and follows CC_STATUS0 for attach,
  power role, orientation, Rp level and VCONN; the data role follows
  PD_STATUS1 on a DataRole interrupt and drives the connector's USB role
  switch (dwc3 through the OF graph);
- sources VBUS through "vbus-supply" (the charger's usb-otg-vbus
  regulator) only while the CCIC reports a sink partner, drops it on
  detach, PR swap and hard reset, and never loops on a refusal: the
  charger returns -EBUSY while CHGIN is present;
- as a sink, on PSRDY reads the source capabilities (0x30), sets the
  charger input current limit the contract allows, and requests (0x32)
  the fixed PDO giving the most power at no more than 9 V, 3 A and 15 W.
  The input limit is lowered to what both contracts allow before asking
  for more voltage and raised after the next PSRDY confirms it; a
  refusal or a hard reset restores it, and a hard reset right after a
  request stops further requests for a minute;
- at probe, and again when SYSMsg reports a CCIC watchdog or power-on
  reset, writes the interrupt masks stock uses, turns firmware VBUS
  control off (0x54), sets FW_AUTOIBUS to 1 (0x57, the host owns the
  input limit) and programs the sink capabilities (0x2e) from the
  connector's sink-pdos, fixed 5-9 V entries only;
- registers a USB power supply reporting online, type, voltage and
  maximum current.

The safety rules of ADR-003 are enforced in code, not left to the device
tree: the mailbox refuses every opcode outside an allowlist of five with
checked payloads, so the firmware-update entry (0xd0 at 0x21), the RAM
test (0xd1), PPS and APDO requests (0x3a, 0x3c, 0x3b) and the ADC
self-test (0x59) cannot be sent; 0x32 is accepted only for a fixed PDO
of 5 to 9 V; the regmap cannot write outside the mask registers and the
mailbox window, so the CCIC reset at 0x80 is unreachable. There is no
firmware update path and no sysfs beyond the typec and power supply
classes.

Without a "maxim,charger" reference the sink never asks for more than
5 V, since it could not lower the input limit first. The protocol is
taken from Samsung's vendor driver (drivers/usb/typec/maxim/, cited by
file and line in the source). Unverified on hardware at the time of
writing: the build has not been booted.

